<section class="shell band">
  <p class="eyebrow">Blog &middot; October 2026</p>
  <h1>Is equine-assisted therapy covered by insurance?</h1>
  <div class="answer">
    <p>No. Equine sessions at this practice are paid directly, and that is the usual position
    across the field. The reason is duller than people expect: it is not that insurers have
    examined the evidence and ruled against it, it is that there is no billing code for it.
    Health plans pay against codes, and equine-assisted psychotherapy does not have one of its
    own.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="shell band">
  <h2>What actually gets billed</h2>
  {% bandArt "/assets/img/horse-grazing.jpg", 480, 640, "A horse grazing in a paddock near Dripping Springs, Texas.", "inset" %}
  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <p>When a licensed clinician provides psychotherapy, that psychotherapy has a code,
      whether it happens in an office, on a video call, or standing in an arena. What does not
      have a code is everything that makes an equine session an equine session: the facility,
      the horses, the extra time, and in many programs a second person handling the animals.</p>
      <p>So a plan looking at a claim sees a psychotherapy session. It does not see, and will
      not pay for, the rest of what the session costs to run. That gap is the reason most
      practices offering this work ask to be paid directly.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>Session length is the other half of it. A standard outpatient psychotherapy benefit is
      built around roughly a fifty-minute hour. Equine work does not compress into that
      usefully. Getting to the property, meeting the horse and letting something actually
      happen takes longer, which is why sessions here run an hour and a half to two hours.</p>
      <p>A plan that pays for fifty minutes does not pay more because the session was longer.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--sand">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2>What to ask your plan</h2>
    <p>You do not have to take anyone else's word for it, including ours. If you want to check
    your own plan, these are the questions that get a usable answer. Ask your insurer directly,
    and write down who you spoke to and when.</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>Does my plan cover outpatient psychotherapy with a Licensed Professional Counselor,
      and what is my cost for it?</li>
      <li>Does my plan exclude equine-assisted, animal-assisted or adventure-based therapy by
      name?</li>
      <li>If I see an out-of-network provider, will you reimburse against a superbill, and at
      what percentage of what allowed amount?</li>
      <li>Is there a session length limit or a limit on sessions per year?</li>
      <li>Do I need prior authorization?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The second question is the one that decides it. Plenty of plans cover counseling and
    separately exclude this delivery format, and the two answers can both be yes without
    contradicting each other.</p>
  </div>
</section>

