<section class="shell band faq">
  <h2>Questions about cost and coverage</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>Is equine-assisted therapy covered by insurance?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No. Equine sessions here are paid directly and are not billed to insurance, and that
      is the usual position elsewhere too. It is not a judgment about whether the work helps.
      There is no billing code specific to equine-assisted psychotherapy, so a plan sees either
      a standard psychotherapy claim or nothing it recognizes, and many plans exclude
      animal-assisted formats by name as well.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Why is there no insurance code for equine therapy?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Codes exist for procedures that have been defined, standardized and submitted for
      inclusion. Equine-assisted work covers several different models with different training
      routes and different methods, which makes it hard to define as one procedure. Until that
      settles, it is billed, if at all, as the psychotherapy inside it.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Can I use out-of-network benefits for an equine session?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>That depends entirely on your plan, and it is worth asking directly: whether they
      reimburse against a superbill, at what percentage, and against which allowed amount. Ask
      before booking rather than after, and get the answer in writing if you can.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Is equine-assisted therapy the same as therapeutic riding?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No, and the difference matters for coverage as well as for what happens. Therapeutic
      riding is an adaptive sport and physical activity. Equine-assisted psychotherapy is
      psychotherapy delivered by a licensed mental health clinician, on the ground, with the
      horse as part of the work rather than as something to ride.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
